  Prefill calibration: how this PC reads prompts fastest (once, for a setup from before it)
  The engine reads a ~4K-token text with a few settings - the chunk of tokens a step takes, and the matrix
  products' library - and keeps the fastest for this PC. One start of the model (two where most experts stay
  in RAM: a larger chunk's decode is checked against the default's); a few minutes.
    chunk 4096, BLAS GEMMs: 61.7 tok/s
    chunk 4096, hipBLASLt GEMMs: 61.7 tok/s
    chunk 1024, BLAS GEMMs: 43.1 tok/s
    chunk 2048, BLAS GEMMs: 51.4 tok/s
    chunk 4096, BLAS GEMMs: 61.7 tok/s (the defaults 43.1) - kept for this PC in /data/minimax-xs/pack/prefill-tune.txt
  [ok] prefill calibration: chunks of 4096 tokens, the BLAS's matrix products - 62 tokens/s reading a 4096-token prompt (+43% over the defaults' 43); kept for this PC

  Speed calibration: the DSpark drafter or none? (once, for a setup that drafts with MiniMax's DSpark drafter)
  Drafting makes some PCs faster and others slower: it depends on how much of the model fits in the GPUs'
  memory, and on the CPU, the RAM and the SSD. So Maya measures both here, the way you will use it. It loads
  the model twice; each time it warms up, then writes about 1024 tokens of answers, and the sustained
  speeds are compared. About 10-30 minutes; the PC is busy.
